    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d741">

                <pb facs="0698" n="526" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg526"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/292a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-02-08T15:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 8, 1917, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4417. Over five weeks have elapsed since the presentation to the Foreign Office
                    of Department’s 4520, December 1, 1916,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d741-f1"/> on the
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Ausable</hi> and other cases, without any indication
                    as to the attitude of the British Government. Is not the time now opportune to
                    make the proposal of arbitration mentioned in my instruction? I dislike to
                    believe that the British Government would hesitate to adjust cases so similar as
                    these are to the <hi rendition="#italic">Trent</hi> case.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
